    "knowledge_point": "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components",
    "problem": "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\sqrt{2+t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3+t}\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k.$",
    "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{2+t}$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{3+t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $1+{t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/01998bcc54d297275af928b2467e7bcc077c84fd595567f5b3ff5bf89d38ec7b.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$",
